<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1129048" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box.png" alt width="1419" height="1109" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box.png 1419w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box-360x281.png 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box-1024x800.png 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box-240x188.png 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/11/25221615/Retro-Swanson-Turkey-Dinner-Box-768x600.png 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1419px) 100vw, 1419px"></p>
<p>“Taste — the mouth’s response to the sensation of eating and drinking — is a strange and complex and complicated thing,” <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/1976/11/22/archives/westchester-weekly-what-taste-means-to-one-man-in-the-kitchen.html?eafs_enabled=false">writes New York&nbsp;<em>Times</em> food editor Craig Claiborne</a>. “I am convinced that there are a multitude of people (Americans, mostly, to judge by the people who buy TV dinners and frozen pizzas) who have no taste whatsoever.” That column was published almost exactly 50 years ago, but even at that point, such convenience-oriented food products had already enjoyed prominence in American life for two decades. Though it could hardly have made a dent in the popularity of TV dinners, Claiborne’s typically waspish aside did draw a <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/1977/01/12/archives/westchester-weekly-letters.html">letter of objection</a> from one Rosalie Levine of Lakewood, California.</p>
<p>“I live alone, I must control my weight. I am not a skilled cook,” she writes. The TV dinner’s price “cannot possibly be bettered”; what’s more, “each oven-ready dinner takes only half an hour’s worth of gas to prepare, and there are no pots or pans to clean up afterward.” While none of these advantages were accidental, the oft-told story of the concept’s emergence begins with a corporate crisis.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>“In 1953, someone at Swanson colossally miscalculated the level of the American appetite for Thanksgiving turkey, leaving the company with some 260 tons of frozen birds,” <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/tray-bon-96872641/">as Smithsonian.com’s Owen Edwards tells it</a>. “Inspired by the trays of pre-prepared food served on airlines,” Swanson salesman Gerry Thomas set the machinery in motion to package the unwanted meat with gravy and sides like cornbread and sweet potatoes, ready for stocking in America’s frozen-food aisles.</p>
